  ('natural-flavouring', 'UIL-0019', 'Natural Flavouring', 'Natural flavour; natural flavourings; natural flavouring substances', 'Flavouring ingredients', 'Natural-source flavour materials', 'A legally defined flavouring category derived from natural source materials using permitted physical, enzymatic or microbiological processes. The term does not mean minimally processed or nutritionally valuable.', 'Plant, animal or microbiological source materials, depending on the flavouring.', 'Creates, restores or standardises flavour and aroma.', 'Additive/functional ingredient', 'Usually nutritionally negligible at the amounts used.', 'Negligible', 'Negligible', 'Negligible', 'Negligible', 'Usually little direct effect.', 'Insufficient information to assign a general gut effect because natural flavouring is a category covering many different substances and preparations.', 'Insufficient ingredient-specific evidence. Any conclusion requires the identity and dose of the actual flavouring substance or preparation.', 'Allergy relevance is source and formulation dependent. The generic term does not justify a blanket allergy or intolerance warning beyond checking declared allergens and known personal sensitivities.', 'People with confirmed relevant allergies or sensitivities; vegans or vegetarians when the source is not clear and source matters to them.', 'Drinks; yoghurts; snacks; cereals; desserts; sauces; supplements.', 'Natural flavouring; natural flavour; natural flavourings', 'No universal replacement is necessary. If greater transparency is preferred, choose products that name the flavour source more specifically.', 'Source dependent', 'Source dependent', 'Usually no inherent gluten, but carriers and source materials require product-level checking.', 'Commonly used in NOVA 4 foods', 'Source-specific flavouring category — details may be limited', 'Insufficient', 'Flavourings are regulated in Great Britain. Authorisation requirements and conditions depend on the specific flavouring category and substance.', 'Natural flavouring describes where flavour materials started, not how simple the finished ingredient is. Without the specific substance, meaningful health conclusions are limited.', 'natural flavouring|natural flavourings|natural flavour|natural flavors|natural flavouring substances', 'natural flavoring; natual flavouring', 'Flavouring; flavouring preparation; extracts', 'Carrier additives may be present', 'https://data.food.gov.uk/regulated-products/flavouring_authorisations/guidance | https://www.gov.uk/food-labelling-and-packaging/ingredients-list | https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/food-allergen-labelling-and-information-requirements-technical-guidance/food-allergen-labelling-and-information-requirements-technical-guidance', '2026-08-08', '{}'::jsonb),
  ('smoke-flavouring', 'UIL-0020', 'Smoke Flavouring', 'Smoke flavour; liquid smoke; smoke flavouring preparation', 'Flavouring ingredients', 'Wood smoke condensate', 'A flavouring produced by capturing and processing smoke from controlled wood combustion, rather than smoking the food directly.', 'Controlled wood smoke condensates and their fractions.', 'Adds smoky aroma and taste; standardises flavour; may reduce the need for direct smoking.', 'Additive/functional ingredient', 'Nutritionally negligible at normal use levels.', 'Negligible', 'Negligible', 'Negligible', 'Negligible', 'Usually little direct effect.', 'Insufficient evidence to assign a general gut effect to the category; composition and exposure depend on the specific authorised smoke flavouring preparation.', 'Insufficient evidence for a general inflammation claim. Safety assessment and permitted uses are preparation-specific.', 'No universal allergen conclusion can be assigned to the category. Check the exact preparation, carriers and finished-product allergen declaration.', 'People with a relevant declared allergy or known flavouring sensitivity; consumers who want to distinguish added smoke flavouring from traditional smoking.', 'Meats; cheese; sauces; crisps; plant-based meat alternatives; seasonings.', 'Smoke flavouring; smoke flavour; liquid smoke; smoke flavouring preparation', 'No universal substitute is nutritionally necessary; choose according to flavour preference and the composition of the finished food.', 'Source dependent', 'Source dependent', 'Usually no inherent gluten; check carriers and final product.', 'Commonly used in NOVA 4 foods', 'Regulated smoke flavouring — preparation-specific', 'Moderate', 'Smoke flavourings are regulated in Great Britain and authorisations are preparation-specific. The current FSA/FSS smoke-flavouring register is the primary GB source for authorised products and conditions of use.', 'Smoke flavouring gives a controlled smoky taste without traditional smoking. It should be assessed by the exact authorised preparation, not by broad assumptions about the word ''smoke''.', 'smoke flavouring|smoke flavoring|smoke flavour|liquid smoke|smoke flavouring preparation', 'smoke flavor; smoke flovouring', 'Natural flavouring; smoked ingredients; grill flavour', 'Carrier additives may be present', 'https://data.food.gov.uk/regulated-products/smoke_authorisations/guidance | https://www.gov.uk/food-labelling-and-packaging/ingredients-list | https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/food-allergen-labelling-and-information-requirements-technical-guidance/food-allergen-labelling-and-information-requirements-technical-guidance', '2026-08-08', '{}'::jsonb),
  ('sucrose', 'UIL-0021', 'Sucrose', 'Table sugar; cane sugar; beet sugar; granulated sugar', 'Sugars and syrups', 'Sugar', 'A disaccharide made from glucose and fructose.', 'Usually extracted and refined from sugar cane or sugar beet.', 'Sweetens, adds bulk, supports browning and affects texture and preservation.', 'Refined ingredient', 'Rapidly available carbohydrate and energy.', 'None', 'None', 'High; effectively 100% added sugar when used as an isolated ingredient.', 'None', 'Raises blood glucose; response depends on amount and the rest of the meal.', 'Usually well tolerated, but large intakes may worsen symptoms in some people.', 'No ingredient-specific inflammatory effect is established; concern relates mainly to high habitual free-sugar intake.', 'Not one of the 14 UK-regulated allergens.', 'People managing diabetes, dental health or overall free-sugar intake.', 'Confectionery, baked goods, drinks, sauces, cereals and desserts.', 'Sugar; sucrose; cane sugar; beet sugar', 'Use less added sweetness; whole fruit where practical.', 'Yes', 'Yes', 'Naturally gluten-free; cross-contamination depends on the product.', 'Context dependent', 'Refined sweetener', 'High', 'Permitted food ingredient; must be declared by name in the ingredients list.', 'A familiar refined sugar. The amount and frequency matter more than the name: it adds sweetness and energy but no fibre, protein or meaningful micronutrient density.', 'sucrose|table sugar|cane sugar|beet sugar|granulated sugar', 'sucrosee|sucros', 'Glucose; fructose; invert sugar syrup', 'None', 'https://www.efsa.europa.eu/en/efsajournal/pub/7074; https://www.gov.uk/food-labelling-and-packaging/ingredients-list; https://www.fao.org/3/ca5644en/ca5644en.pdf', '2026-08-04', '{}'::jsonb),
  ('fructose', 'UIL-0022', 'Fructose', 'Fruit sugar; crystalline fructose', 'Sugars and syrups', 'Monosaccharide', 'A single sugar molecule naturally present in fruit and honey and also used as a refined sweetener.', 'Commercial fructose is commonly produced from starch-derived glucose syrups.', 'Provides sweetness, moisture retention and browning.', 'Refined ingredient', 'Rapidly available carbohydrate and energy.', 'None', 'None', 'High when added in isolated form; naturally occurring fructose in whole fruit comes with fibre and water.', 'None when isolated', 'Usually produces a lower immediate glucose rise than glucose, but high intakes still add substantial free sugar.', 'Large amounts can cause bloating or diarrhoea in people with fructose malabsorption.', 'No direct ingredient-specific inflammatory effect is established; excessive free-sugar intake is the relevant concern.', 'Fructose malabsorption and hereditary fructose intolerance require different levels of caution.', 'People with fructose malabsorption, hereditary fructose intolerance, diabetes or high free-sugar intake.', 'Soft drinks, sports products, confectionery, baked goods and flavoured foods.', 'Fructose; crystalline fructose; fruit sugar', 'Whole fruit rather than isolated fructose for sweetness and fibre.', 'Yes', 'Yes', 'Gluten-free as a pure ingredient.', 'Context dependent', 'Refined sweetener', 'High', 'Permitted food ingredient; labelling must not mislead consumers about fruit content.', 'Fructose in whole fruit is not the same nutritional package as isolated fructose. The isolated form adds concentrated sweetness without the fibre and structure of fruit.', 'fructose|crystalline fructose|fruit sugar', 'fructos|frutose', 'Glucose; sucrose; agave syrup', 'None', 'https://www.efsa.europa.eu/en/efsajournal/pub/7074; https://www.gov.uk/food-labelling-and-packaging/ingredients-list; https://www.fao.org/3/ca5644en/ca5644en.pdf', '2026-08-04', '{}'::jsonb),
  ('high-fructose-corn-syrup', 'UIL-0023', 'High-fructose corn syrup', 'HFCS; glucose-fructose corn syrup; corn syrup with high fructose', 'Sugars and syrups', 'Glucose-fructose syrup', 'A corn-starch-derived syrup in which some glucose has been converted to fructose.', 'Industrial processing of maize starch.', 'Sweetens beverages and foods, retains moisture and is easy to blend.', 'Highly refined ingredient', 'Rapidly available carbohydrate and energy.', 'None', 'None', 'High; an added free-sugar syrup.', 'None', 'Raises blood glucose and contributes free sugars; effect depends on composition and dose.', 'Usually tolerated, but large amounts may worsen symptoms in fructose-sensitive people.', 'Evidence does not show it is uniquely harmful compared with similar amounts of other free sugars; high intake remains a concern.', 'Not one of the 14 UK-regulated allergens.', 'People monitoring free sugars, diabetes, dental health or fructose-related symptoms.', 'Imported drinks, confectionery, sauces, baked goods and sweetened dairy-style products.', 'High-fructose corn syrup; HFCS; glucose-fructose syrup', 'Water or unsweetened drinks; products with less total added sugar.', 'Yes', 'Yes', 'Gluten-free as a pure ingredient.', 'Commonly used in NOVA 4 foods', 'Industrial free-sugar syrup', 'High', 'Permitted ingredient; naming can vary by formulation and market.', 'HFCS is an industrial free-sugar syrup, not a special poison. Its main relevance is how easily it increases the total sugar load of highly formulated foods and drinks.', 'High-fructose corn syrup; high fructose corn syrup; hfcs; corn glucose fructose syrup; glucose fructose corn syrup', 'high frutose corn syrup|hfc syrup', 'Glucose-fructose syrup; fructose; glucose syrup', 'None', 'https://www.efsa.europa.eu/en/efsajournal/pub/7074; https://www.gov.uk/food-labelling-and-packaging/ingredients-list; https://www.fao.org/3/ca5644en/ca5644en.pdf', '2026-08-04', '{}'::jsonb),
  ('golden-syrup', 'UIL-0024', 'Golden syrup', 'Light treacle; inverted sugar syrup', 'Sugars and syrups', 'Invert sugar syrup', 'A thick amber syrup containing sucrose, glucose and fructose, traditionally made during sugar refining.', 'Sugar cane or beet refining and inversion.', 'Adds sweetness, colour, moisture and characteristic flavour.', 'Refined ingredient', 'Rapidly available carbohydrate and energy.', 'None', 'None', 'High; predominantly free sugars.', 'None', 'Raises blood glucose; response depends on portion and meal context.', 'Generally well tolerated; high intakes can cause gastrointestinal discomfort.', 'No unique inflammatory effect is established beyond concerns linked to high free-sugar intake.', 'Not one of the 14 UK-regulated allergens.', 'People monitoring diabetes, dental health or free-sugar intake.', 'Baking, flapjacks, desserts, confectionery and sauces.', 'Golden syrup; light treacle', 'Use smaller amounts; fruit purees where suitable for the recipe.', 'Yes', 'Yes', 'Gluten-free as a pure ingredient.', 'Context dependent', 'Refined sweetener', 'High', 'Permitted food ingredient.', 'Golden syrup is still concentrated free sugar despite its colour and traditional image. It provides flavour and texture, not fibre or meaningful nutrition.', 'golden syrup|light treacle|inverted sugar syrup', 'golden sirup|golden syrop', 'Invert sugar syrup; sucrose; glucose syrup', 'None', 'https://www.efsa.europa.eu/en/efsajournal/pub/7074; https://www.gov.uk/food-labelling-and-packaging/ingredients-list; https://www.fao.org/3/ca5644en/ca5644en.pdf', '2026-08-04', '{}'::jsonb),
