  ('barley-malt-extract', 'UIL-0026', 'Barley malt extract', 'Malt extract; barley malt syrup; malt syrup', 'Sugars and syrups', 'Barley', 'A sweet extract produced from malted barley, containing maltose and flavour compounds.', 'Barley grains that are germinated, dried, mashed and concentrated.', 'Adds sweetness, colour, malt flavour and supports fermentation.', 'Processed ingredient', 'Mostly carbohydrate, with small variable amounts of other barley-derived compounds.', 'Low', 'Low', 'Moderate to high; concentrated malt sugars.', 'Low', 'Can raise blood glucose.', 'Usually tolerated, but unsuitable for people requiring strict gluten avoidance unless specifically certified and compliant.', 'No unique inflammatory effect is established; gluten is the key concern for coeliac disease.', 'Barley is a regulated cereal allergen and contains gluten.', 'People with coeliac disease, barley allergy, diabetes or those monitoring added sugars.', 'Cereals, breads, malt drinks, biscuits, confectionery and brewing.', 'Barley malt extract; malt extract (barley); barley malt syrup', 'Gluten-free certified alternatives such as rice syrup where functionally suitable.', 'Yes', 'Yes', 'Contains barley-derived gluten unless a compliant gluten-free claim is present.', 'Context dependent', 'Malted cereal sweetener', 'High', 'Barley must be emphasised as an allergen in UK ingredient lists.', 'Barley malt extract adds flavour and sweetness, but it is not suitable for routine gluten-free use. Its main nutritional contribution is concentrated carbohydrate.', 'barley malt extract|malt extract|barley malt syrup|malt syrup', 'barley mal extract|malt extact', 'Brown rice syrup; maltose; glucose syrup', 'None', 'https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/food-allergen-labelling-and-information-requirements-technical-guidance/food-allergen-labelling-and-information-requirements-technical-guidance; https://www.gov.uk/food-labelling-and-packaging/ingredients-list; https://www.fao.org/3/ca5644en/ca5644en.pdf', '2026-08-04', '{}'::jsonb),
  ('honey', 'UIL-0027', 'Honey', 'Raw honey; clear honey; set honey', 'Sugars and syrups', 'Bee product', 'A naturally produced sweet substance made by bees from floral nectar or secretions.', 'Bees; composition varies by floral source and processing.', 'Sweetens, adds flavour, binds moisture and supports browning.', 'Processed ingredient', 'Mostly glucose and fructose, with water and small amounts of other compounds.', 'Negligible', 'Negligible', 'High; honey counts as free sugar in dietary guidance.', 'Negligible', 'Raises blood glucose, though the response varies by composition and portion.', 'Usually tolerated; can trigger symptoms in rare cases and must not be given to infants under 12 months due to botulism risk.', 'No reliable evidence supports treating ordinary culinary amounts as anti-inflammatory medicine.', 'Rare pollen or bee-product reactions are possible; unsuitable for infants under 12 months.', 'People managing free sugars or diabetes; parents of infants; strict vegans.', 'Drinks, yoghurt, cereals, sauces, bakery products and spreads.', 'Honey; raw honey; blossom honey; acacia honey', 'Whole fruit for sweetness; smaller portions.', 'No', 'Yes', 'Naturally gluten-free; contamination depends on the finished product.', 'Usually NOVA 2', 'Natural free-sugar sweetener', 'High', 'Honey is a food ingredient; infant botulism advice remains important.', 'Honey is natural, but it is still concentrated free sugar. Its small amounts of bioactive compounds do not cancel out the sugar load.', 'honey|raw honey|clear honey|set honey|blossom honey', 'huny|honee', 'Fructose; glucose; agave syrup', 'None', 'https://www.efsa.europa.eu/en/efsajournal/pub/7074; https://www.gov.uk/food-labelling-and-packaging/ingredients-list; https://www.fao.org/3/ca5644en/ca5644en.pdf', '2026-08-04', '{}'::jsonb),
  ('agave-syrup', 'UIL-0028', 'Agave syrup', 'Agave nectar; agave sweetener', 'Sugars and syrups', 'Agave plant', 'A concentrated syrup from agave plants, usually processed to produce a fructose-rich sweetener.', 'Agave sap or agave carbohydrate extracts processed and concentrated.', 'Sweetens foods and drinks and is marketed as a plant-based alternative to sugar or honey.', 'Highly refined ingredient', 'Rapidly available carbohydrate and energy.', 'None', 'None', 'High; concentrated free sugar, often fructose-rich.', 'None', 'May produce a lower immediate glucose rise than glucose-rich syrups, but remains a concentrated added sugar.', 'Can worsen symptoms in people with fructose malabsorption when consumed in larger amounts.', 'No unique anti-inflammatory advantage is established over other free sugars.', 'Not one of the 14 UK-regulated allergens.', 'People with fructose malabsorption, hereditary fructose intolerance, diabetes or high free-sugar intake.', 'Vegan desserts, drinks, snack bars, sauces and ''natural'' products.', 'Agave syrup; agave nectar', 'Use less sweetener; whole fruit where practical.', 'Yes', 'Yes', 'Gluten-free as a pure ingredient.', 'Context dependent', 'Fructose-rich refined sweetener', 'High', 'Permitted food ingredient; ''nectar'' wording does not change its free-sugar status.', 'Agave is plant-based, but it is not a low-sugar health food. It is a concentrated sweetener, commonly rich in fructose and easy to overuse.', 'agave syrup|agave nectar|agave sweetener', 'agavae syrup|agave nector', 'Fructose; honey; sucrose', 'None', 'https://www.efsa.europa.eu/en/efsajournal/pub/7074; https://www.gov.uk/food-labelling-and-packaging/ingredients-list; https://www.fao.org/3/ca5644en/ca5644en.pdf', '2026-08-04', '{}'::jsonb),
  ('coconut-sugar', 'UIL-0029', 'Coconut sugar', 'Coconut palm sugar; coconut blossom sugar', 'Sugars and syrups', 'Coconut palm sap', 'A granulated sweetener made by concentrating sap from coconut palm flowers.', 'Coconut palm flower sap, heated and crystallised.', 'Sweetens and adds caramel-like flavour; often marketed as less refined.', 'Processed culinary ingredient', 'Mostly sucrose with smaller amounts of glucose and fructose.', 'Negligible', 'Negligible', 'High; concentrated free sugar.', 'Very low', 'Raises blood glucose; any trace minerals do not make it nutritionally equivalent to a whole food.', 'Usually well tolerated; high intakes may cause digestive discomfort.', 'No unique anti-inflammatory benefit is established.', 'Not the same as coconut flesh; coconut allergy is possible but uncommon.', 'People managing free sugars, diabetes or coconut allergy.', 'Baking, drinks, snack products and vegan foods.', 'Coconut sugar; coconut palm sugar; coconut blossom sugar', 'Use less added sugar; whole fruit where suitable.', 'Yes', 'Yes', 'Gluten-free as a pure ingredient.', 'Context dependent', 'Alternative refined sugar', 'Moderate', 'Permitted food ingredient; nutrition and health claims must comply with applicable rules.', 'Coconut sugar sounds more wholesome, but it remains mostly sugar. Trace minerals do not meaningfully change its role as a concentrated sweetener.', 'coconut sugar|coconut palm sugar|coconut blossom sugar', 'cocoanut sugar|coconut suger', 'Sucrose; palm sugar; brown sugar', 'None', 'https://www.efsa.europa.eu/en/efsajournal/pub/7074; https://www.gov.uk/food-labelling-and-packaging/ingredients-list; https://www.fao.org/3/ca5644en/ca5644en.pdf', '2026-08-04', '{}'::jsonb),
  ('maple-syrup', 'UIL-0030', 'Maple syrup', 'Pure maple syrup; maple sap syrup', 'Sugars and syrups', 'Maple tree sap', 'A concentrated syrup made by boiling or evaporating maple tree sap.', 'Sap from maple trees, concentrated by heat or evaporation.', 'Sweetens and adds distinctive flavour.', 'Processed culinary ingredient', 'Mostly sucrose and water, with small amounts of minerals and flavour compounds.', 'Negligible', 'Negligible', 'High; concentrated free sugar.', 'Negligible', 'Raises blood glucose; impact depends on portion and meal composition.', 'Usually well tolerated.', 'No unique anti-inflammatory benefit is established at normal culinary intakes.', 'Not one of the 14 UK-regulated allergens.', 'People monitoring free sugars, diabetes or dental health.', 'Pancakes, baking, cereals, sauces and desserts.', 'Maple syrup; pure maple syrup', 'Use smaller amounts; fruit-based sweetness where practical.', 'Yes', 'Yes', 'Gluten-free as a pure ingredient.', 'Usually NOVA 2', 'Natural free-sugar sweetener', 'High', 'Permitted food ingredient; products described as maple syrup should not be misleadingly substituted with flavoured sugar syrups.', 'Pure maple syrup is less industrial than many syrups, but nutritionally it is still concentrated free sugar. Its flavour may help people use less, but portion size remains key.', 'maple syrup|pure maple syrup|maple sap syrup', 'mapel syrup|maple sirup', 'Sucrose; golden syrup; honey', 'None', 'https://www.efsa.europa.eu/en/efsajournal/pub/7074; https://www.gov.uk/food-labelling-and-packaging/ingredients-list; https://www.fao.org/3/ca5644en/ca5644en.pdf', '2026-08-04', '{}'::jsonb),
  ('potato-starch', 'UIL-0031', 'Potato starch', 'Potato flour starch; native potato starch', 'Starches and thickeners', 'Potato', 'The purified starch fraction extracted from potatoes.', 'Potatoes washed, crushed and separated to isolate starch granules.', 'Thickens, binds water, improves crispness and modifies texture.', 'Refined ingredient', 'Digestible carbohydrate unless specifically modified or resistant.', 'None', 'None', 'High starch; little sugar.', 'Very low unless resistant starch is specified', 'Cooked starch can raise blood glucose; response varies with processing, cooling and food structure.', 'Generally well tolerated; large amounts may feel heavy or cause bloating in some people.', 'No ingredient-specific inflammatory effect is established.', 'Not one of the 14 UK-regulated allergens; rare potato allergy exists.', 'People managing blood glucose or potato allergy.', 'Soups, sauces, gluten-free baking, crisps, noodles and processed meats.', 'Potato starch; native potato starch', 'Whole potato or less-refined thickeners where suitable; choice depends on function.', 'Yes', 'Yes', 'Naturally gluten-free; verify cross-contamination for coeliac use.', 'Context dependent', 'Refined starch', 'High', 'Permitted ingredient; if chemically modified it may be labelled as modified starch with source where allergen-relevant.', 'Potato starch is not the same as eating a potato. It mainly supplies purified carbohydrate and texture, with most of the original food matrix removed.', 'potato starch|native potato starch|potato flour starch', 'potato startch|potatoe starch', 'Modified starch; resistant starch; tapioca starch', 'Modified starch additives where applicable', 'https://www.gov.uk/food-labelling-and-packaging/ingredients-list; https://www.fao.org/3/ca5644en/ca5644en.pdf', '2026-08-04', '{}'::jsonb),
  ('tapioca-starch', 'UIL-0032', 'Tapioca starch', 'Cassava starch; tapioca flour', 'Starches and thickeners', 'Cassava', 'A purified starch extracted from cassava root.', 'Cassava roots processed to remove fibre and isolate starch.', 'Thickens, binds, adds chew and supports gluten-free texture.', 'Refined ingredient', 'Mostly digestible carbohydrate.', 'None', 'None', 'High starch; little sugar.', 'Very low', 'Usually raises blood glucose when cooked; effect depends on food structure and portion.', 'Generally well tolerated; large portions may cause digestive discomfort.', 'No ingredient-specific inflammatory effect is established.', 'Not one of the 14 UK-regulated allergens; rare cassava allergy is possible.', 'People monitoring blood glucose or relying heavily on refined gluten-free starches.', 'Gluten-free bread, puddings, pearls, sauces, snacks and vegan cheese alternatives.', 'Tapioca starch; cassava starch; tapioca flour', 'Whole-food starch sources or higher-fibre flours where function allows.', 'Yes', 'Yes', 'Naturally gluten-free; manufacturing cross-contamination remains possible.', 'Context dependent', 'Refined starch', 'High', 'Permitted ingredient; properly processed food-grade cassava ingredients are required.', 'Tapioca starch is useful for texture and gluten-free cooking, but it contributes mainly refined carbohydrate rather than fibre, protein or micronutrients.', 'tapioca starch|cassava starch|tapioca flour', 'tapioca startch|tapicoa starch', 'Potato starch; maize starch; modified starch', 'Modified starch additives where applicable', 'https://www.gov.uk/food-labelling-and-packaging/ingredients-list; https://www.fao.org/3/ca5644en/ca5644en.pdf', '2026-08-04', '{}'::jsonb),
  ('wheat-starch', 'UIL-0033', 'Wheat starch', 'Native wheat starch; wheat-derived starch', 'Starches and thickeners', 'Wheat', 'The purified starch fraction separated from wheat flour.', 'Wheat grain processed to separate starch from proteins and other components.', 'Thickens, binds and modifies texture.', 'Refined ingredient', 'Mostly carbohydrate.', 'Very low after separation, but residual wheat proteins may remain.', 'None', 'High starch; little sugar.', 'Very low', 'Can raise blood glucose depending on preparation and portion.', 'Usually tolerated outside wheat-related disorders.', 'No general inflammatory effect is established; gluten or wheat proteins are the relevant issue for affected people.', 'Wheat is a regulated allergen. Ordinary wheat starch is not automatically safe for coeliac disease; specially processed gluten-free wheat starch may meet legal limits but can still matter for wheat allergy.', 'People with coeliac disease, wheat allergy or diabetes.', 'Sauces, soups, bakery goods, processed meats and some gluten-free-labelled foods using specially processed wheat starch.', 'Wheat starch; starch (wheat); gluten-free wheat starch', 'Certified gluten-free starches where required; potato, maize or tapioca starch depending on use.', 'Yes', 'Yes', 'Wheat-derived. Only use as gluten-free when the finished product legally meets the gluten-free threshold; wheat allergy remains separate.', 'Context dependent', 'Refined allergen-derived starch', 'High', 'Wheat must generally be emphasised as an allergen. Gluten-free claims require the finished food to meet legal limits.', 'Wheat starch is mostly carbohydrate, but its wheat origin matters. Coeliac safety and wheat-allergy safety are not identical, so the actual label and certification must be checked.', 'wheat starch|native wheat starch|gluten free wheat starch|starch wheat', 'wheat startch|weat starch', 'Modified starch; potato starch; maize starch', 'Modified starch additives where applicable', 'https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/food-allergen-labelling-and-information-requirements-technical-guidance/food-allergen-labelling-and-information-requirements-technical-guidance; https://www.gov.uk/food-labelling-and-packaging/ingredients-list; https://www.fao.org/3/ca5644en/ca5644en.pdf', '2026-08-04', '{}'::jsonb),
  ('resistant-starch', 'UIL-0034', 'Resistant starch', 'RS1; RS2; RS3; RS4; retrograded starch', 'Fibres and resistant carbohydrates', 'Starch', 'Starch that resists digestion in the small intestine and reaches the large bowel, where some forms are fermented.', 'Occurs naturally in some foods and can also be produced through processing, cooling or chemical modification.', 'Adds fibre-like functionality, can reduce available carbohydrate and may improve texture.', 'Processed ingredient', 'Resistant carbohydrate functioning partly like dietary fibre.', 'None', 'None', 'Lower digestible carbohydrate than ordinary starch, depending on type and dose.', 'Moderate to high depending on specification', 'Usually produces a lower glucose response than an equal amount of fully digestible starch.', 'Can support fermentation and short-chain fatty acid production; may cause gas or bloating when increased quickly.', 'Potential benefits are plausible and supported for some forms, but effects vary by type, dose and food matrix.', 'Not inherently a regulated allergen; source ingredients may be allergenic.', 'People with sensitive digestion, IBS or those increasing fibre rapidly; source-specific allergy concerns.', 'High-fibre foods, cooled starch foods, breads, bars, powders and reformulated products.', 'Resistant starch; resistant maize starch; retrograded starch; RS2; RS3; RS4', 'Whole legumes, intact grains, cooled potatoes or rice where tolerated.', 'Source dependent', 'Usually', 'Depends on source; wheat-derived resistant starch may require allergen attention.', 'Context dependent', 'Functional resistant carbohydrate', 'Moderate', 'Health claims depend on the specific resistant starch and conditions of use; source allergen rules still apply.', 'Resistant starch is not one single ingredient. Some forms occur naturally, while others are manufactured. It can behave more like fibre than ordinary starch, but gut effects and benefits depend on the exact type and amount.', 'resistant starch|resistant maize starch|retrograded starch|rs1|rs2|rs3|rs4', 'resistent starch|resistant startch', 'Potato starch; maize starch; dietary fibre', 'Modified starch additives where applicable', 'https://www.efsa.europa.eu/en/efsajournal/pub/1462; https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/food-allergen-labelling-and-information-requirements-technical-guidance/food-allergen-labelling-and-information-requirements-technical-guidance; https://www.fao.org/3/ca5644en/ca5644en.pdf', '2026-08-04', '{}'::jsonb),
  ('inulin', 'UIL-0035', 'Inulin', 'Chicory inulin; fructan; long-chain inulin', 'Fibres and resistant carbohydrates', 'Fructan fibre', 'A soluble fermentable carbohydrate made mainly of linked fructose units.', 'Naturally present in plants such as chicory, onion and garlic; commercial inulin is often extracted from chicory root.', 'Adds fibre, bulk, mild sweetness and creamy texture; can replace some fat or sugar.', 'Additive/functional ingredient', 'Dietary fibre with little direct energy compared with digestible carbohydrate.', 'None', 'None', 'Low available sugar contribution in typical use.', 'High', 'Usually has little direct effect on blood glucose.', 'Fermented by gut microbes; may support beneficial bacteria but can cause gas, pain or bloating, especially in IBS or FODMAP sensitivity.', 'No universal anti-inflammatory effect is established; microbiome responses vary.', 'Not one of the 14 UK-regulated allergens.', 'People with IBS, fructan/FODMAP sensitivity or those new to added fibre.', 'Fibre bars, yoghurts, cereals, protein products, bakery goods and supplements.', 'Inulin; chicory inulin; fructan; oligofructose-inulin', 'Whole vegetables, legumes, oats and other fibre sources as tolerated.', 'Yes', 'Yes', 'Gluten-free as a pure ingredient.', 'Context dependent', 'Fermentable added fibre', 'High', 'Permitted ingredient; nutrition or health claims must meet specific legal conditions.', 'Inulin can increase fibre without adding much sugar, but it is highly fermentable. Helpful for some people, uncomfortable for others—especially when a large dose is added suddenly.', 'inulin|chicory inulin|fructan|long chain inulin', 'innulin|inuline', 'Chicory root fibre; oligofructose; FOS', 'None', 'https://www.efsa.europa.eu/en/efsajournal/pub/1462; https://www.gov.uk/food-labelling-and-packaging/ingredients-list; https://www.fao.org/3/ca5644en/ca5644en.pdf', '2026-08-04', '{}'::jsonb),
  ('chicory-root-fibre', 'UIL-0036', 'Chicory root fibre', 'Chicory fibre; chicory root extract; chicory inulin', 'Fibres and resistant carbohydrates', 'Chicory root', 'A fibre-rich ingredient extracted from chicory root, usually containing inulin and shorter-chain fructans.', 'Chicory roots processed with water extraction and purification.', 'Adds fibre, bulk, mild sweetness and texture; helps reduce sugar or fat in formulations.', 'Additive/functional ingredient', 'Dietary fibre.', 'None', 'None', 'Low available sugar contribution, though composition varies.', 'High', 'Generally low direct glycaemic impact.', 'Highly fermentable; may cause gas, bloating, urgency or pain in sensitive people.', 'No universal anti-inflammatory effect is established.', 'Not one of the 14 UK-regulated allergens.', 'People with IBS, fructan/FODMAP sensitivity or sensitivity to concentrated added fibres.', 'Snack bars, cereals, yoghurts, protein foods, bakery products and supplements.', 'Chicory root fibre; chicory fibre; chicory root extract; inulin', 'Whole-food fibre sources and gradual fibre increases.', 'Yes', 'Yes', 'Gluten-free as a pure ingredient.', 'Commonly used in NOVA 4 foods', 'Concentrated fermentable fibre', 'High', 'Permitted ingredient; claims depend on composition and amount.', 'Chicory root fibre can boost a product''s fibre number, but it may not feel the same as fibre from intact food. Its strong fermentability makes dose and individual tolerance important.', 'chicory root fibre|chicory fiber|chicory root extract|chicory inulin', 'chicory root fiber|chicory root fibber', 'Inulin; oligofructose; FOS', 'None', 'https://www.efsa.europa.eu/en/efsajournal/pub/1462; https://www.gov.uk/food-labelling-and-packaging/ingredients-list; https://www.fao.org/3/ca5644en/ca5644en.pdf', '2026-08-04', '{}'::jsonb),
  ('polydextrose', 'UIL-0037', 'Polydextrose', 'E1200; poly-d-glucose', 'Fibres and resistant carbohydrates', 'Synthetic glucose polymer', 'A manufactured, poorly digested glucose polymer used as a bulking ingredient and fibre source.', 'Produced by polymerising glucose with small amounts of other permitted components.', 'Adds bulk, body and fibre while reducing sugar or energy density.', 'Additive/functional ingredient', 'Low-energy fermentable carbohydrate counted as fibre under applicable definitions.', 'None', 'None', 'Low digestible sugar contribution.', 'Moderate to high', 'Usually has a low direct glycaemic effect.', 'Partly fermented; larger amounts can cause gas, bloating or laxative effects.', 'No specific inflammatory effect is established.', 'Not one of the 14 UK-regulated allergens.', 'People with sensitive digestion or consuming several fibre-enriched products.', 'Low-sugar confectionery, bars, desserts, drinks and meal replacements.', 'Polydextrose; E1200; poly-d-glucose', 'Whole-food fibre sources where practical.', 'Usually', 'Usually', 'Gluten-free as a pure ingredient.', 'Commonly used in NOVA 4 foods', 'Industrial added fibre / bulking agent', 'High', 'Authorised as food additive E1200 and used under applicable conditions; high intakes may require advisory wording depending on product and jurisdiction.', 'Polydextrose can lower sugar and add fibre-like bulk, but it is still an industrial formulation tool. Its main practical issue is digestive tolerance when several enriched products are eaten together.', 'polydextrose|e1200|poly d glucose', 'polydexrose|poly dextrose', 'Inulin; soluble corn fibre; maltodextrin', 'E1200', 'https://www.efsa.europa.eu/en/efsajournal/pub/1462; https://www.gov.uk/food-labelling-and-packaging/ingredients-list; https://www.fao.org/3/ca5644en/ca5644en.pdf', '2026-08-04', '{}'::jsonb),
  ('oat-fibre', 'UIL-0038', 'Oat fibre', 'Oat hull fibre; oat dietary fibre', 'Fibres and resistant carbohydrates', 'Oats', 'A concentrated fibre ingredient produced from oat fractions, often rich in insoluble fibre.', 'Oat hulls or other oat fractions processed and milled; composition varies by supplier.', 'Adds fibre, bulk, water binding and texture.', 'Refined ingredient', 'Mostly dietary fibre; beta-glucan content may be low unless specifically stated.', 'Low', 'Low', 'Low available carbohydrate depending on specification.', 'High', 'Usually has little direct glycaemic effect, but the finished product matters.', 'Can support stool bulk; rapid increases may cause bloating.', 'No universal anti-inflammatory effect is established.', 'Oats are a regulated cereal allergen for labelling. Cross-contact with wheat, barley or rye is important for coeliac disease unless certified gluten-free.', 'People with oat allergy, coeliac disease or sensitive digestion.', 'High-fibre breads, bars, cereals, meat alternatives and bakery products.', 'Oat fibre; oat fiber; oat hull fibre', 'Whole oats or oat bran when suitable, as they retain more of the food matrix.', 'Yes', 'Yes', 'Oats are naturally gluten-free by composition but commonly contaminated; only certified gluten-free oat products are suitable for many people with coeliac disease.', 'Context dependent', 'Concentrated cereal fibre', 'High', 'Oats must be declared and emphasised as an allergen; gluten-free claims require compliance.', 'Oat fibre is not the same as whole oats. It can raise fibre content, but it may contain less beta-glucan and fewer nutrients than intact oats or oat bran.', 'oat fibre|oat fiber|oat hull fibre|oat dietary fibre', 'oat fibber|oat fiibre', 'Oat bran; beta-glucan; cellulose', 'None', 'https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/food-allergen-labelling-and-information-requirements-technical-guidance/food-allergen-labelling-and-information-requirements-technical-guidance; https://www.efsa.europa.eu/en/efsajournal/pub/1462; https://www.fao.org/3/ca5644en/ca5644en.pdf', '2026-08-04', '{}'::jsonb),
  ('cellulose', 'UIL-0039', 'Cellulose', 'Powdered cellulose; plant cellulose; microcrystalline cellulose', 'Fibres and resistant carbohydrates', 'Plant cell walls', 'A structural carbohydrate from plant cell walls that humans do not digest.', 'Wood pulp, cotton or other plant materials purified for food use; form varies.', 'Adds fibre, bulk, anti-caking, moisture control and texture.', 'Highly refined ingredient', 'Insoluble dietary fibre with minimal available energy.', 'None', 'None', 'Negligible available carbohydrate.', 'High', 'Little direct effect on blood glucose.', 'Generally not fermented much; can increase stool bulk. Large amounts may cause fullness or digestive discomfort.', 'No ingredient-specific inflammatory effect is established.', 'Not one of the 14 UK-regulated allergens.', 'People sensitive to high added-fibre loads or with swallowing/intestinal narrowing disorders should follow clinical advice.', 'Bakery products, grated cheese, low-calorie foods, supplements, sauces and meat alternatives.', 'Cellulose; powdered cellulose; microcrystalline cellulose; plant fibre', 'Whole vegetables, legumes, grains and seeds for a broader nutrient package.', 'Usually', 'Usually', 'Gluten-free as a pure ingredient.', 'Context dependent', 'Industrial insoluble fibre / texture agent', 'High', 'Some forms are authorised additives, including microcrystalline cellulose E460; labelling depends on function and formulation.', 'Cellulose is the same type of structural fibre found in plants, but isolated cellulose is mainly used for bulk and texture. It can add fibre without adding the wider nutrients of the original plant.', 'cellulose|powdered cellulose|microcrystalline cellulose|plant cellulose', 'cellolose|cellullose', 'Oat fibre; insoluble fibre; methylcellulose', 'E460', 'https://www.efsa.europa.eu/en/efsajournal/pub/1462; https://www.gov.uk/food-labelling-and-packaging/ingredients-list; https://www.fao.org/3/ca5644en/ca5644en.pdf', '2026-08-04', '{}'::jsonb),
  ('psyllium-husk', 'UIL-0040', 'Psyllium husk', 'Ispaghula husk; psyllium fibre; Plantago ovata husk', 'Fibres and resistant carbohydrates', 'Plantago ovata seed husk', 'A soluble, gel-forming fibre from the outer husk of Plantago ovata seeds.', 'Seed husks cleaned, dried and milled.', 'Adds fibre, absorbs water, thickens and supports gluten-free structure.', 'Refined ingredient', 'Viscous soluble fibre.', 'Low', 'Low', 'Low available carbohydrate.', 'High', 'Can slow carbohydrate absorption and reduce post-meal glucose response when used appropriately.', 'Supports stool formation and regularity; insufficient fluid can cause obstruction, and rapid increases may cause bloating.', 'Potential metabolic benefits are supported, but it should not be presented as a cure or anti-inflammatory treatment.', 'Rare allergy is possible, particularly with occupational exposure to psyllium dust.', 'People with swallowing difficulty, bowel narrowing, severe gut symptoms, medication timing concerns or psyllium allergy.', 'Fibre supplements, gluten-free bread, cereals and high-fibre foods.', 'Psyllium husk; ispaghula husk; psyllium fibre; Plantago ovata', 'Whole-food fibre variety; psyllium remains useful when clinically appropriate.', 'Yes', 'Yes', 'Naturally gluten-free; cross-contamination depends on manufacturing.', 'Context dependent', 'Functional gel-forming fibre', 'High', 'Permitted food ingredient; product directions and adequate fluid intake are important. It can affect absorption timing of some medicines.', 'Psyllium is a useful, well-supported fibre rather than a filler to dismiss. Its benefits depend on enough water, suitable dosing and individual medical circumstances.', 'psyllium husk|ispaghula husk|psyllium fibre|plantago ovata', 'psyllum husk|psillium husk|ispagula', 'Soluble fibre; oat beta-glucan; inulin', 'None', 'https://www.efsa.europa.eu/en/efsajournal/pub/1462; https://www.gov.uk/food-labelling-and-packaging/ingredients-list; https://www.fao.org/3/ca5644en/ca5644en.pdf', '2026-08-04', '{}'::jsonb),
  ('coconut-oil', 'UIL-0041', 'Coconut Oil', 'Coconut fat; copra oil', 'Oils and fats', 'Coconut', 'An edible fat extracted from coconut flesh, naturally high in saturated fatty acids.', 'Pressed or solvent-extracted from fresh or dried coconut meat, then sold virgin or refined.', 'Provides fat, texture, mouthfeel and heat stability; used in cooking and food manufacture.', 'Refined ingredient', 'Almost entirely fat; predominantly saturated fat.', 'None', 'High; rich in saturated fat.', 'None', 'None', 'Does not directly raise blood glucose, but adds substantial energy.', 'Usually well tolerated; large amounts can cause digestive discomfort.', 'No evidence that coconut oil is uniquely anti-inflammatory; replacing unsaturated oils with it can increase saturated-fat intake.', 'Coconut allergy is uncommon but possible; coconut is not one of the 14 UK-regulated allergens.', 'People managing saturated-fat intake, blood lipids, coconut allergy or total energy intake.', 'Cooking fats, bakery products, confectionery, vegan products, fillings and coatings.', 'Coconut oil; coconut fat; virgin coconut oil; refined coconut oil', 'For everyday use, oils richer in unsaturated fat such as extra virgin olive or rapeseed oil.', 'Yes', 'Yes', 'Naturally gluten-free; cross-contamination depends on manufacturing.', 'Context dependent', 'Saturated plant fat', 'Moderate', 'Permitted food ingredient; nutrition claims and allergen communication must follow UK rules.', 'Coconut oil is a plant fat, but that does not make it nutritionally equivalent to olive or rapeseed oil. Its saturated-fat content is the key point.', 'coconut oil|coconut fat|copra oil|virgin coconut oil|refined coconut oil', 'cocoanut oil|cocunut oil', 'Palm oil; cocoa butter; medium-chain triglyceride oil', 'None inherently', 'https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/composition-of-foods-integrated-dataset-cofid | https://www.efsa.europa.eu/en/topics/topic/dietary-reference-values | https://www.food.gov.uk/business-guidanceindustry-specific-advice/allergen-labelling', '2026-08-04', '{}'::jsonb),
  ('soybean-oil', 'UIL-0042', 'Soybean Oil', 'Soya oil; soy oil', 'Oils and fats', 'Soybean', 'A vegetable oil extracted from soybeans, usually refined for neutral flavour and stability.', 'Soybeans; commonly solvent-extracted and refined, though expeller-pressed versions exist.', 'Provides fat, carries flavours and supports frying, dressings, spreads and processed-food texture.', 'Refined ingredient', 'Almost entirely fat; generally rich in polyunsaturated fat, including omega-6 linoleic acid.', 'None', 'High; mostly unsaturated fat.', 'None', 'None', 'No direct carbohydrate effect.', 'Usually well tolerated; large fat loads may worsen symptoms in some people.', 'Soybean oil is not established as inherently inflammatory; overall fatty-acid balance, total diet and replacement context matter.', 'Soy is a regulated UK allergen. Highly refined soybean oil may be exempt from allergen labelling in specific circumstances, but cold-pressed oil may retain more protein.', 'People with soy allergy, those monitoring overall fat quality and anyone relying on allergen-free claims.', 'Mayonnaise, dressings, margarines, bakery products, sauces and fried foods.', 'Soybean oil; soya oil; soy oil', 'Rapeseed or olive oil where their flavour and use are suitable.', 'Yes', 'Yes', 'Naturally gluten-free.', 'Context dependent', 'Refined unsaturated vegetable oil', 'High', 'Soy allergen rules depend on the degree of refining and applicable exemptions; product labels remain decisive.', 'Soybean oil is mostly unsaturated fat. The important distinction is the degree of refining and whether soy-allergy labelling exemptions apply.', 'soybean oil|soya oil|soy oil|refined soybean oil|cold pressed soybean oil', 'soyabean oil|soy bean oil', 'Soya lecithin; soybean protein; vegetable oil', 'Soya lecithin (E322) may be related but is a different ingredient', 'https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/composition-of-foods-integrated-dataset-cofid | https://www.efsa.europa.eu/en/topics/topic/dietary-