b),
  ('oat-flour', 'UIL-0088', 'Oat Flour', 'Ground oats; whole oat flour; milled oats', 'Cereal flours', 'Oats', 'Flour made by finely milling oats, usually retaining much of the whole grain.', 'Oat grain.', 'Adds softness, mild flavour, water binding and fibre to baked goods and breakfast foods.', 'Whole/minimally processed', 'Mostly carbohydrate with useful beta-glucan fibre, moderate protein and some unsaturated fat.', 'Moderate.', 'Moderate for a cereal flour, mainly unsaturated.', 'High.', 'Moderate to high, including soluble beta-glucan.', 'Often moderate; fine milling can increase availability compared with intact oats.', 'Beta-glucan can support regularity; rapid fibre increases may cause gas or bloating.', 'No general inflammatory classification; some people with coeliac disease also react to oat avenin.', 'Oats are a regulated allergen category; cross-contamination with wheat, barley or rye is important for coeliac disease.', 'People with oat allergy, coeliac disease, or those requiring certified gluten-free oats.', 'Porridge products, pancakes, biscuits, bars, breads and gluten-free-style baking.', 'Oat flour; ground oats; milled oats', 'Certified gluten-free oat flour for coeliac diets where oats are tolerated; less finely milled oats for more texture.', 'Yes', 'Yes', 'Oats must still be declared; only specially controlled products can be labelled gluten-free within legal limits.', 'Usually NOVA 1', 'Wholegrain cereal flour', 'High', 'Oats must be emphasised as an allergen even when labelled gluten-free.', 'Oat flour retains useful oat fibre, but fine milling changes texture and digestion. Coeliac-safe use requires properly controlled gluten-free oats.', 'oat flour|ground oats|milled oats', 'oat flower|oats flour', 'Rolled oats; oat bran; oat fibre', 'None inherent', 'https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/food-allergen-labelling-and-information-requirements-technical-guidance/food-allergen-labelling-and-information-requirements-technical-guidance; htt