  ('mycoprotein', 'UIL-0236', 'Mycoprotein', 'Fungal protein; Fusarium venenatum mycoprotein; fermented fungal biomass', 'Plant proteins and meat alternatives', 'Fungal biomass', 'Protein- and fibre-containing food biomass produced by fermenting an approved filamentous fungus, most commonly Fusarium venenatum in established UK products. It is fungal, not a plant protein.', 'Cultivated fungal biomass grown by fermentation.', 'Provides structure, protein and fibre in meat-alternative foods.', 'Processed ingredient', 'Provides protein and fibre with relatively little fat before other formulation ingredients are added.', 'Moderate to high; amino-acid quality is generally useful but product formulation varies.', 'Low in the plain ingredient; finished products may contain added oils.', 'Low to moderate.', 'Moderate.', 'Usually low direct impact.', 'Fibre may support fullness but can cause bloating in some people; rare adverse reactions are reported.', 'No general inflammatory effect established.', 'Allergic reactions can occur. Many commercial mycoprotein products also contain egg or milk, but these are not inherent to all mycoprotein.', 'People with known mycoprotein or mould-related allergy concerns, and vegans checking egg or milk in finished products.', 'Meat-free mince; burgers; nuggets; sausages; ready meals.', 'Mycoprotein; fungal protein; fermented fungal protein.', 'Beans, lentils, tofu, tempeh or minimally formulated mycoprotein products depending on needs.', 'Formulation dependent', 'Formulation dependent', 'Naturally gluten-free as an ingredient; finished products may contain wheat', 'Context dependent', 'Fermented fungal protein; assess the finished product', 'High', 'Authorisation and labelling depend on the specific fungal biomass and market status; allergens in finished products must be declared.', 'Mycoprotein can provide protein and fibre, but the burger or nugget around it may add oils, coatings, salt and allergens. It is fungal protein, not plant protein.', 'mycoprotein; fungal protein; fusarium venenatum; fermented fungal biomass', 'myco protein; microprotein', 'Fungal biomass; textured vegetable protein; tempeh', 'Binders, flavourings, egg, milk, wheat and stabilisers may occur in finished products', 'https://www.efsa.europa.eu/en/topics/topic/novel-food; https://www.gov.uk/food-labelling-and-packaging/ingredients-list; https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/food-allergen-labelling-and-information-requirements-technical-guidance/food-allergen-labelling-and-information-requirements-technical-guidance', '2026-08-05', '{}'::jsonb),
  ('jerusalem-artichoke-fibre', 'UIL-0237', 'Jerusalem Artichoke Fibre', 'Jerusalem artichoke inulin; sunchoke fibre; topinambur fibre', 'Fibres and prebiotic ingredients', 'Jerusalem artichoke', 'A fibre-rich ingredient obtained from Jerusalem artichoke tubers, commonly concentrated for their inulin-type fructans.', 'Helianthus tuberosus tubers.', 'Adds fibre, mild sweetness, bulk and prebiotic functionality.', 'Processed ingredient', 'Mainly fermentable fibre, especially inulin-type fructans; composition varies.', 'Negligible.', 'Negligible.', 'Low digestible carbohydrate when purified, though whole-tuber powders may contain more carbohydrate.', 'High.', 'Usually low direct impact when fibre-rich; exact product composition matters.', 'Highly fermentable and can cause gas, bloating, pain or diarrhoea in people sensitive to fructans/FODMAPs.', 'No general inflammatory effect established; tolerance is individual.', 'Not one of the 14 regulated allergens; rare individual allergy is possible.', 'People with IBS, fructan sensitivity or those introducing concentrated prebiotic fibre.', 'Fibre bars; cereals; yoghurts; bakery products; supplements; meat alternatives.', 'Jerusalem artichoke fibre; Jerusalem artichoke inulin; sunchoke fibre.', 'Whole vegetables, oats, legumes or lower-dose fibres as tolerated.', 'Yes', 'Yes', 'Naturally gluten-free; check blends', 'Context dependent', 'Fermentable prebiotic fibre; child profile of inulin', 'High', 'Permitted as a food ingredient; any nutrition or health claim must meet applicable conditions.', 'Jerusalem artichoke fibre can raise fibre intake, but it is strongly fermentable. Helpful for some people, uncomfortable for others.', 'jerusalem artichoke fibre; sunchoke fibre; topinambur fibre; jerusalem artichoke inulin', 'jerusalem artichock fibre; artichoke fiber', 'Inulin; chicory root fibre; fructooligosaccharides', 'None inherent', 'https://www.efsa.europa.eu/en/efsajournal/pub/1462; https://www.gov.uk/food-labelling-and-pack