  ('beet-sugar', 'UIL-0254', 'Beet Sugar', 'Sugar beet sugar; white beet sugar; sucrose from beet', 'Sugars, syrups and sweetening ingredients', 'Sucrose', 'Crystalline sucrose extracted and refined from sugar beet. Chemically it is essentially the same sucrose as refined cane sugar.', 'Sugar beet root.', 'Sweetens; adds bulk; supports browning, texture and preservation.', 'Processed culinary ingredient', 'Added/free sugar and energy.', 'None.', 'None.', 'High sugar contribution.', 'None.', 'High when consumed alone; the whole food matrix matters.', 'No fibre; excessive intake can displace fibre-rich foods and contribute to dental decay.', 'No unique inflammatory property compared with cane sucrose; excess added sugar is the relevant issue.', 'Not one of the 14 regulated allergens.', 'People managing diabetes, dental health or total free-sugar intake.', 'Confectionery, bakery, drinks, sauces, cereals and preserves.', 'Beet sugar; sugar; sucrose', 'Use less added sugar; whole fruit or minimally sweetened products where suitable.', 'Yes', 'Yes', 'Gluten-free.', 'Context dependent', 'Red/amber — refined added sugar', 'High', 'Usually labelled simply as sugar; claims should not imply meaningful health superiority over cane sugar.', 'Beet sugar and cane sugar are the same main molecule: sucrose. The amount matters more than which crop it came from.', 'beet sugar|sugar beet sugar|sucrose from beet', 'beat sugar|beet suger', 'Cane Sugar; Sucrose; White Sugar', 'None inherently.', 'https://www.gov.uk/food-labelling-and-packaging/nutrition-health-claims-and-supplement-labelling; https://www.gov.uk/food-labelling-and-packaging/ingredients-list', '2026-08-05', '{}'::jsonb),
  ('chickpea-protein', 'UIL-0255', 'Chickpea Protein', 'Chickpea protein concentrate; chickpea protein isolate; garbanzo protein', 'Legumes and pulse derivatives', 'Chickpea', 'A protein-rich fraction separated from chickpeas. Concentrates and isolates differ in protein content, fibre and processing.', 'Milled chickpeas processed by dry or wet fractionation.', 'Raises protein; improves structure, binding, emulsification and texture.', 'Processed ingredient', 'Protein; residual carbohydrate and fibre vary.', 'High relative to whole chickpeas, but amino-acid balance and digestibility differ from animal proteins.', 'Usually low.', 'Lower starch than whole chickpeas; residual carbohydrate varies.', 'Lower than whole chickpeas in many isolates; concentrates may retain some fibre.', 'Usually low to moderate depending on residual starch and product.', 'May cause gas or bloating in sensitive people; purified forms may contain fewer fermentable carbohydrates than whole chickpeas.', 'No general anti-inflammatory effect established.', 'Chickpea allergy is possible and can cross-react in some people with other legumes. Not one of the 14 named UK allergens.', 'People with chickpea or legume allergy and those sensitive to pulse proteins.', 'Meat alternatives, protein powders, bakery, pasta and snack products.', 'Chickpea protein; garbanzo protein; chickpea protein isolate; chickpea protein concentrate', 'Whole chickpeas or less refined pulse ingredients when texture allows.', 'Yes', 'Yes', 'No inherent gluten; cross-contact must be checked.', 'Often associated with NOVA 3', 'Amber — useful protein, more refined than the whole pulse', 'Moderate', 'Not a regulated named allergen, but allergen information must still be accurate and product-specific.', 'Chickpea protein can add useful protein, but it removes much of the whole pulse structure. Check the finished product, not just the protein headline.', 'chickpea protein|garbanzo protein|chickpea protein isolate|chickpea protein conce