  ('cassava', 'UIL-0433', 'Cassava', 'Yuca; manioc; cassava root; Manihot esculenta', 'Vegetable ingredients', 'Cassava root', 'The starchy root of Manihot esculenta, used as a staple food after appropriate processing and preparation.', 'Cassava roots intended for human food; cultivar, cyanogenic content, processing and supplied form vary.', 'Starchy food, bulk and texture in meals and processed root-based foods.', 'Whole/minimally processed', 'Mainly starch carbohydrate and energy; it is not a substantial protein ingredient.', 'Low in protein relative to protein foods; cassava root is different from cassava leaves.', 'Low inherent fat in plain root; frying or added fat changes this.', 'Mainly starch, with composition and digestibility affected by preparation.', 'Some root fibre; peeling and conversion to isolated starch change the amount.', 'Contributes carbohydrate; portion, processing and the meal affect response. No fixed glycaemic index assigned.', 'No established generic gut-treatment benefit assigned; safe processing must be addressed before nutritional use.', 'No inflammation rating assigned: this generic profile does not establish an inflammatory or anti-inflammatory effect in people.', 'Cassava is not one of the 14 regulated allergens. Cyanogenic compounds are a preparation-related toxicity issue, separate from food allergy.', 'Anyone preparing cassava must avoid raw or inadequately processed material and use a verified food-grade product with appropriate preparation instructions.', 'Prepared cassava dishes, root-based foods, snacks and ingredients subsequently made into cassava flour or starch.', 'Cassava; Yuca; manioc; cassava root; Manihot esculenta', 'Another appropriately prepared starchy food where preferred; no universal reason to reject properly processed food-grade cassava.', 'Vegan', 'Vegetarian', 'No inherent gluten in the plain ingredient; carriers, blends and cross-contact need separate assessment.', 'Usually NOVA 1', 'Starchy root requiring verified safe processing and preparation', 'High', 'General food-safety obligations apply. Australian/New Zealand preparation guidance is used here as safety evidence, not as a statement of GB-specific labelling law.', 'Cassava is a starchy root used as food after appropriate processing. Its natural cyanogenic compounds make safe sourcing and preparation essential; raw cassava should not be eaten.', 'cassava|cassava root|yuca|manioc|manihot esculenta', 'None specifically validated; spelling corrections must not override exact ingredient/allergen matching.', 'Cassava Flour (UIL-0459); Cassava Starch (UIL-0539); Tapioca Starch', 'None inherent; assess the full formulation.', 'https://www.foodstandards.gov.au/consumer/prevention-of-foodborne-illness/cassava | https://www.foodstandards.gov.au/sites/default/files/publications/Documents/28_Cyanogenic_glycosides.pdf | https://www.foodauthority.nsw.gov.au/media/2996', '2026-10-15', '{}'::jsonb),
  ('wheat-sourdough', 'UIL-0434', 'Wheat Sourdough', 'Fermented wheat dough; wheat sour dough', 'Cereal and grain derivatives', 'Wheat flour', 'A wheat-flour preparation fermented with sourdough microorganisms. Wet, dried and heat-treated commercial forms differ.', 'Wheat flour and water fermented with lactic acid bacteria and yeasts; flour type, culture, fermentation and added ingredients vary.', 'Fermented flavour, acidity and dough characteristics; leavening contribution depends on whether the preparation is active.', 'Processed ingredient', 'Mainly cereal carbohydrate, with some protein and fibre depending on flour type and formulation.', 'Contains wheat protein, including gluten in ordinary preparations; it is not a protein isolate.', 'Usually little in a plain flour-and-water base; additional fats change the recipe.', 'Contains wheat starch; fermentation does not by itself establish a low-carbohydrate food.', 'Depends on whether refined or wholegrain flour is used; sourdough does not automatically mean wholegrain or high fibre.', 'No fixed glycaemic index or glucose-control benefit assigned; flour, fermentation, portion and the finished food matter.', 'Ordinary sourdough is not automatically probiotic or low-FODMAP; strain, fermentation, baking and the actual product need separate evidence.', 'No inflammation rating assigned: this generic profile does not establish an inflammatory or anti-inflammatory effect in people.', 'Contains WHEAT, a regulated allergen. Other cereals, milk or added ingredients depend on the recipe.', 'People with wheat allergy or coeliac disease, or requiring a verified low-FODMAP product rather than a general sourdough description.', 'Sourdough breads, bakery mixes, fermented doughs and flavouring or starter ingredients for baking.', 'Wheat Sourdough; Fermented wheat dough; wheat sour dough', 'A verified gluten-free formulation for coeliac needs, or a wheat-free alternative for wheat allergy; no general reason to replace suitable sourdough.', 'Usually vegan', 'Usually vegetarian', 'Ordinary wheat sourdough contains gluten. Fermentation alone does not establish gluten-free compliance or wheat-allergy suitability.', 'Context dependent', 'Fermented cereal ingredient requiring wheat, flour and preparation context', 'Moderate', 'Declare and emphasise wheat. Gluten-free and health claims require product-specific compliance; a sourdough description alone is insufficient.', 'Wheat sourdough is a fermented wheat preparation. It can affect flavour and baking performance, but it remains a wheat ingredient and is not automatically gluten-free, low-FODMAP or probiotic.', 'wheat sourdough|wheat sour dough|fermented wheat dough', 'None specifically validated; spelling corrections must not override exact ingredient/allergen matching.', 'Sourdough Culture (UIL-0346); Sourdough Starter (UIL-0501); Wheat Sourdough Starter (UIL-0553); Rye Sourdough Starter (UIL-0527)', 'None inherent; assess the full formulation.', 'https://www.lesaffre.com/trends-mag/sourdough-ferment-at-the-heart-of-global-baking-trends/ | https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/food-allergen-labelling-and-information-requirements-technical-guidance/food-allergen-labelling-and-information-requirements-technical-guidance | https://www.coeliac.org.uk/about-coeliac-disease/bsg-guideline-on-the-diagnosis-and-management-of-adult-coeliac-disease-the-highlights/', '2026-10-15', '{}'::jsonb),
  ('modified-rice-starch', 'UIL-0435', 'Modified Rice Starch', 'Modified rice-derived starch', 'Starches and carbohydrate ingredients', 'Rice starch', 'Rice starch altered to change its performance in food. The rice source does not identify the modification process or an additive number.', 'Starch isolated from rice and modified to a specified grade; botanical source, treatment and function need supplier documentation.', 'Thickening, texture, gelling or stability in formulated foods.', 'Additive/functional ingredient', 'Primarily starch carbohydrate; it is different from whole rice, rice flour or rice protein.', 'Not a meaningful protein ingredient; purification and residual protein vary by grade.', 'Usually little inherent fat; any blend requires assessment.', 'Mainly starch; digestibility depends on the modification and preparation.', 'Not automatically dietary fibre or resistant starch. Require grade-specific analytical evidence before a fibre claim.', 'Actual carbohydrate exposure depends on grade, amount and cooking; no fixed glycaemic index or glucose-lowering effect assigned.', 'No generic prebiotic or gut-treatment benefit established by the word modified.', 'No inflammation rating assigned: this generic profile does not establish an inflammatory or anti-inflammatory effect in people.', 'Rice is not one of the 14 regulated allergens, though individual rice allergy can occur; processing and blends require separate checks.', 'People needing a verified starch grade, allergen information or substantiated fibre/resistant-starch content.', 'Sauces, soups, desserts, bakery, dairy or plant-based formulations and other texture-controlled foods.', 'Modified Rice Starch; Modified rice-derived starch', 'Another starch or thickener only if it meets the technical requirement; no general reason to replace an appropriate authorised grade.', 'Vegan', 'Vegetarian', 'No inherent gluten in the plain ingredient; carriers, blends and cross-contact need separate assessment.', 'Commonly used in NOVA 4 foods', 'Functional starch requiring exact modification and grade context', 'Moderate', 'Chemically modified additive starches require the relevant authorisation/use conditions. Rice origin alone does not determine an E-number; physical treatments can have a different regulatory context.', 'Modified rice starch helps control food texture. Its name does not establish resistant starch, a health benefit, an E-number or genetic-modification status.', 'modified rice starch|modified rice-derived starch', 'None specifically validated; spelling corrections must not override exact ingredient/allergen matching.', 'Rice Starch; Rice Flour; Modified Pea Starch (UIL-0409); Resistant Starch', 'A specific chemically modified starch may have an E-number, but no code or modification chemistry is assigned without the exact grade.', 'https://www.ingredion.com/emea/en-uk/ingredients/ingredient-types/starches/modified-starch | https://www.ingredion.com/na/en-us/ingredients/ingredient-types/rice-starches-and-flours | https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/food-additives-authorisation-guidance/food-additives-authorisation-guidance', '2026-10-15', '{}'::jsonb),
  ('liquid-sugar', 'UIL-0436', 'Liquid Sugar', 'Liquid sucrose; sucrose solution; sugar solution; sucrose syrup', 'Sugars, syrups and sweetening ingredients', 'Sucrose', 'Sugar supplied dissolved in water, commonly a sucrose solution. Some commercial liquid-sugar products are partly inverted or blended.', 'Refined sugar, generally from sugar beet or cane, dissolved in water; sugar type, inversion and concentration vary by grade.', 'Sweetness and easy handling, dissolution and dosing in food or beverage manufacture.', 'Refined ingredient', 'Free sugars and energy; water content determines the sugar amount per gram.', 'None in a plain sugar-and-water solution.', 'None in a plain sugar-and-water solution.', 'Mainly sucrose in a standard sucrose solution; inverted or blended grades have different glucose/fructose proportions. Added sugars count as free sugars.', 'None in a plain sugar solution.', 'The sugar amount and finished-food portion affect glucose response; liquid form does not establish a lower-GI or glucose-control advantage.', 'No fibre or established generic gut-treatment benefit.', 'No inflammation rating assigned: this generic profile does not establish an inflammatory or anti-inflammatory effect in people.', 'Plain sucrose and water are not among the 14 regulated allergens; assess any additional ingredients or blends.', 'People monitoring free sugars or carbohydrate intake; use the actual product concentration and serving.', 'Soft drinks, sauces, confectionery, bakery, desserts, dairy or plant-based products and syrups.', 'Liquid Sugar; Liquid sucrose; sucrose solution; sugar solution; sucrose syrup', 'A less-sweet recipe or reduced amount where technically feasible; switching to another free sugar does not remove free sugars.', 'Usually vegan', 'Usually vegetarian', 'No inherent gluten in the plain ingredient; carriers, blends and cross-contact need separate assessment.', 'Usually NOVA 2', 'Culinary sugar solution requiring concentration and portion conte