  ('dill-weed', 'UIL-0533', 'Dill Weed', 'Dill leaf; dill leaves; dried dill weed; fresh dill weed', 'Herbs, spices and seasonings', 'Leafy culinary herb', 'The leafy parts of dill (Anethum graveolens), used fresh or dried; distinct from dill seed and extracted dill flavouring.', 'Dill leaves and fine leafy stems, harvested and supplied fresh, frozen or dried as specified.', 'Herbal flavour in sauces, fish dishes, pickles and savoury foods.', 'Whole/minimally processed', 'Small seasoning quantities usually contribute little to overall macronutrient intake; fresh and dried weights are not directly comparable.', 'Small contribution at ordinary seasoning amounts.', 'Usually a small contribution at seasoning amounts.', 'Leaf carbohydrate contribution is usually small at use levels.', 'Leaf material contains fibre, but a seasoning amount is not a standard fibre-rich serving.', 'No established fixed glycaemic index or blood-glucose-lowering benefit from seasoning use.', 'No established probiotic or gut-treatment benefit from dill weed.', 'No inflammation rating assigned: this generic profile does not establish an inflammatory or anti-inflammatory effect in people.', 'Dill can cause allergy although outside the UK 14 allergens. Botanical relationship to celery does not mean the ingredient contains CELERY.', 'People with dill allergy or checking compound seasoning blends.', 'Fish dishes, sauces, pickles, soups, salads and seasonings.', 'Dill Weed; Dill leaf; dill leaves; dried dill weed; fresh dill weed', 'Another suitable leafy herb; aroma and recipe behaviour differ.', 'Vegan', 'Vegetarian', 'No inherent gluten in the plain ingredient; blends and cross-contact need separate assessment.', 'Usually NOVA 1', 'Fresh or dried leafy herb', 'High', 'Identify dill leaf accurately and assess other ingredients in blends; leaf use does not establish a medicinal claim.', 'Dill weed is the leafy herb, fresh or dried. It is distinct from dill seed and contributes flavour at small use l